Ours (PhoneCanDo), not from llama.cpp. GET /slots also gives the whole
prompt of the request a slot works on, so the app can show how much of it
is still to read.

A first reply on a phone CPU waits minutes while the server reads the
agent's prompt. The runner watches that through /slots (the agent's own
engine sends the request, so we cannot ask for return_progress), and a
slot there gives the new tokens read so far (n_prompt_tokens_processed)
and those its cache held (n_prompt_tokens_cache), but not the prompt's
size: n_prompt_tokens is what the slot holds so far, which grows batch by
batch while it reads.

server_slot::to_json adds n_prompt_tokens_total, the task's own token
count: the same number the streamed prompt_progress gives as total. It is
there whenever the other n_prompt_tokens_* fields are; nothing else
changes.

--- a/tools/server/server-context.cpp
+++ b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
@@ -822,6 +822,9 @@
             res["n_prompt_tokens"]           = (int32_t) prompt.tokens.size();
             res["n_prompt_tokens_processed"] = stats.n_prompt_processed;
             res["n_prompt_tokens_cache"]     = stats.n_prompt_cached;
+            // PhoneCanDo patch: the request's whole prompt, as the streamed
+            // prompt_progress's total (n_prompt_tokens is what the slot holds so far)
+            res["n_prompt_tokens_total"]     = ptask->n_tokens();
             res["params"] = ptask->params.to_json(only_metrics);
             res["next_token"] = json::array({
                 {
